{"name":"Smoky Topaz","hex":"#7e7668","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"smoky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Filled with smoke.","Filled with or enveloped in tobacco smoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoky"},{"term":"topaz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A silicate mineral of aluminium and fluorine, usually tinted by impurities.","An often clear, yellowish-brown gemstone cut from this."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellowish-brown color, like that of the gemstone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/topaz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Topaz","blocks":[{"text":"Topaz is a silicate mineral made of aluminum and fluorine with the chemical formula Al2SiO4(F, OH)2. It is used as a gemstone in jewelry and other adornments. Common topaz in its natural state is colorless, though trace element impurities can make it pale blue or golden-brown to yellow-orange. Topaz is often treated with heat or radiation to make it a deep blue, reddish-orange, pale green, pink, or purple."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg/330px-Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topaz","wikidata":"Q134985"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Topaz.GIF/500px-Topaz.GIF?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Topaz.GIF/500px-Topaz.GIF?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Topaz's crystal structure using polyhedrons showcasing the aluminum octahedron (grey), silica tetrahedron (blue), oxygen (red), and fluorine (green).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATopaz.GIF"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Topaz-k-182a.jpg/5